## 4. Practice Program — Scan and Print a Character

WAP to scan one char from user, and print the char and its ASCII value.


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    char ch;
    printf("Enter char: \n");
    scanf("%c", &ch);
    printf("char: %c \n", ch);
    printf("ascii: %d \n", ch);
    return 0;
}


Sample run:

Enter char: a
char: a
ascii: 97


## ASCII range check example


if (ch >= 'a' && ch <= 'z')
    printf("char: %c ascii: %d \n", ch, ch);



## 5. Practice Program — Student Result (Grading with 'else if')

WAP to scan one student's total marks and display the result of the student.

| Marks Range | Result |
| 0 – 34      | Fail   |
| 35 – 59     | C      |
| 60 – 74     | B      |
| 75 – 100    | A      |


int m;
printf("Enter the marks ");
scanf("%d \n", &m);

if (m >= 75 && m <= 100)              // (m < 35)
    printf("A \n");
else if (m >= 60 && m <= 74)          // (m < 60)
    printf("B \n");
else if (m >= 35 && m <= 59)          // (m < 75)
    printf("C \n");
else
    printf("fail \n");




## 6. Nested 'if'

Nested if = an 'if' statement within another 'if' statement.

## Syntax (style 1 — if/else with nesting)


if (exp1)
{
    if (exp2)
        st1;
    else
        st2;
}
else
{
    if (exp3)
        st3;
    else
        st4;
}


## Syntax (style 2 — deep nesting)


if (exp1)
{
    if (exp2)
    {
        if (exp3)
        {
            if (exp4)
            {
                st1;
            }
        }
        st2;
    }
}




## 7. 'goto' Statement

'goto' is one of the non-iterative, unconditional control statements.

- In C, we use 'local goto' (jump is within the same function only).
- 'goto' can be used in two different ways:
  1.  Forward jump
  2.  Backward jump

## Two things to take care of when using 'goto':
1.  Calling the label:  goto label_name;
2.  Setting the label:  label_name:

>   'label_name' should 'not' be any keyword or constant.

## Example — Forward Jump


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    printf("Hello... \n");
    goto l;                    // calling the label
    printf("Hai1... \n");      // skipped
    printf("Hai2... \n");      // skipped
l:                              // setting the label
    printf("bye... \n");
    return 0;
}


## Example — Backward Jump


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
l:                              // setting the label
    printf("Hello... \n");
    printf("Hai1... \n");      // repeats
    printf("Hai2... \n");
    goto l;                    // calling the label
    printf("bye... \n");
    return 0;
}


Useful shortcut: 'Ctrl + C' → to stop execution (e.g. of an infinite loop in the terminal).



## 8. Practice Program — Multiplication Table using `goto`

 WAP  to print the multiplication table for a given number using 'goto'.

Example:

Input:  num = 5
Output: 5 * 1  = 5
        5 * 2  = 10
        ...
        5 * 10 = 50



#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int num, i;
    printf("enter int num: \n");
    scanf("%d \n", &num);

    i = 1;
l:  printf("%d * %d = %d \n", num, i, num * i);
    i++;
    if (i <= 10)
        goto l;

    return 0;
}




